  That said, there are two ways to sequence this, and the difference matters.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether we build each layer to completion in turn, or build a minimum end-to-end path through all four and
  then deepen it.

  - [1mLayered[0m — finish tasks, then identity, then subscriptions, then notifications. Buys: each spec is clean and
    complete; no rework within a layer. Costs: nothing is demoable until the very end, and the notifications design —
    the thing you actually want — is the one most likely to reveal that an earlier layer chose wrong. Hard to correct
    late.
  - [1mVertical slice[0m — one task type, one change event ("task reassigned"), two hardcoded-ish users, one delivery channel
    (in-app bell), end to end. Then widen: more event types, more interest rules, more channels. Buys: the notification
    path is exercised early, so its constraints inform the task and identity models while they're still cheap to change.
    Costs: you revisit each layer more than once; the first version of each is deliberately thin.
  - [1mNotifications-spec-first[0m — write the notifications design now as a target, then derive the minimum each lower layer
    must provide. Buys: keeps your actual goal in the driver's seat. Costs: it's a spec you can't implement for a while;
    risk of designing against a task model that doesn't survive contact with implementation.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m vertical slice. A notification system's hard problems are all in the seams — what counts as a
  change, who counts as caring, what happens when the same task changes five times in a minute — and you find those by
  running one event end to end, not by perfecting a task CRUD layer first. It also means you see something working
  early, which matters given the whole app is currently an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m.
